{
  "response": "# Verdict: REVISE\n\n## Summary\nThe `FeedbackForm` component establishes solid baseline semantic HTML (native `<form>`, `<label>` with matching `htmlFor`, and appropriate `autoComplete` attributes), but contains significant accessibility design failures in focus management, live region configuration, and post-submission lifecycle handling. While standard automated tools (e.g., axe-core) would pass this code because attributes are syntactically valid, screen reader and keyboard users will experience focus loss to `<body>` on submission, suppressed loading announcements due to misplaced `aria-busy`, and severe cognitive friction caused by a vague success message that leaves the form populated with stale data and the submit button active.\n\n---\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n*Predicted before line-by-line inspection based on the \"Async Form with Status Announcements\" component archetype:*\n1. **Focus Drop on Button Disable**: Disabling `<button type=\"submit\">` while it holds active focus will cause the browser to dump focus to `document.body`.\n2. **Live Region Suppression / Race Condition**: Setting `aria-busy=\"true\"` directly on the live region announcing the loading state will mute or defer the loading message in multiple screen readers.\n3. **Incomplete Error Communication**: Server error handling will broadcast globally without associating errors to inputs via `aria-describedby` or marking fields with `aria-invalid`.\n4. **Vague Post-Submission State**: Form will not reset or swap to a confirmation view, leaving populated inputs below a terse success banner, confusing both assistive technology and cognitive users.\n5. **Inaccessible Focus Styling in Windows High Contrast Mode**: Replacing `outline` with `box-shadow` will cause focus indicators to disappear entirely when forced colors are active.\n\n*Comparison with actual findings:* All five predictions were confirmed directly in the source and styles.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical Findings\n\n1. **Focus Loss to `document.body` on Submit Button Deactivation**\n   - **Evidence**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:111` (`disabled={status === 'submitting'}`) and `FeedbackForm.jsx:11` (`statusRef = useRef(null)`).\n   - **Element**: `<button type=\"submit\" ... disabled={status === 'submitting'}>`\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only and Screen Reader users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order (Level A), WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide \u2014 Form Interaction.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: When a keyboard user activates the submit button (via Enter or Space), that button holds active document focus. Setting `disabled={true}` forces standard browser engines (Chromium, Gecko, WebKit) to immediately purge focus to `document.body`. While `statusRef` was declared and attached to the status `<div>` at `FeedbackForm.jsx:47`, it is never focused programmatically. When the request resolves, keyboard users are stranded at the document root; pressing Tab jumps to the browser chrome or the first link on the host page rather than the status feedback or form.\n   - **Fix**: Either manage focus deliberately to a focusable status container (`statusRef.current?.focus()`) with `tabIndex={-1}`, or avoid native `disabled` on the active trigger by using `aria-disabled=\"true\"` with a submission guard in `handleSubmit`.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: form_focus_loss_on_disabled_submit\nfingerprint: f4c8319e72a01d5b\nsource: FeedbackForm.jsx:111\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 2.4.3 (Level A)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=HIGH, low_vision=MEDIUM, cognitive=HIGH\nevidence: FeedbackForm.jsx:111: disabled={status === 'submitting'}; statusRef attached at line 47 but never receives focus.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Fill in fields. 2. Focus 'Send Feedback' button with Tab. 3. Press Space/Enter. 4. Inspect document.activeElement while submitting.\nexpected_behavior: Focus remains stable or moves intentionally to a designated status container with tabIndex=\"-1\".\nactual_behavior: document.activeElement reverts to document.body, disorienting AT and keyboard users.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n2. **Misplaced `aria-busy` Silences Loading State in Live Region**\n   - **Evidence**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:51` (`aria-busy={status === 'submitting'}`) on the container hosting `FeedbackForm.jsx:54-59`.\n   - **Element**: `<div role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\" aria-busy={status === 'submitting'}>`\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen Reader users (VoiceOver, NVDA, JAWS).\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages (Level AA), WAI-ARIA 1.2 specification (\u00a76.5.1 `aria-busy`).\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: Per the W3C ARIA specification, setting `aria-busy=\"true\"` instructs assistive technologies to withhold processing modifications to that element and its descendants until `aria-busy` returns to `\"false\"`. Placing `aria-busy=\"true\"` on the very container that dynamically receives `<p>Submitting your feedback...</p>` suppresses the announcement while the network call is ongoing. When the call finishes, the text is unmounted during the 200ms `clearing` phase, meaning the user never hears the loading announcement at all.\n   - **Fix**: Remove `aria-busy` from the live region container. `aria-busy=\"true\"` belongs on the content region being updated or fetched (e.g., a data table or search results panel undergoing a mutation), not on the notification region conveying that work is underway.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: live_region_aria_busy_suppression\nfingerprint: a98e032bc4170e91\nsource: FeedbackForm.jsx:51\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 4.1.2 / 4.1.3 (Level AA)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=LOW, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: FeedbackForm.jsx:51: aria-busy={status === 'submitting'} wraps the child {status === 'submitting' && (<p>Submitting...</p>)}.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Enable VoiceOver / NVDA. 2. Submit form. 3. Listen for polite announcement.\nexpected_behavior: Screen reader announces \"Submitting your feedback...\".\nactual_behavior: Screen reader suppresses dynamic child update because parent tree is marked aria-busy=\"true\".\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings\n\n1. **Conflicting Nested Live Regions (`role=\"alert\"` inside `role=\"status\"`)**\n   - **Evidence**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:48-49` (`role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"`) and `FeedbackForm.jsx:66` (`role=\"alert\"`).\n   - **Element**: `<div role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">` containing `<p role=\"alert\">`\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen Reader users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages (Level AA), WAI-ARIA APG Live Region Design.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: `role=\"status\"` has an implicit live politeness of `polite`. `role=\"alert\"` has an implicit live politeness of `assertive`. Nesting an assertive live region inside an active polite live region causes undefined arbitration in screen readers. In JAWS and iOS VoiceOver, nested conflicting live regions can result in announcements being dropped, truncated, or announced twice.\n   - **Fix**: Keep the persistent container with `role=\"status\"` (`aria-live=\"polite\"`), or render the alert in a separate dedicated error container outside the polite status region.\n\n2. **Vague Post-Submission State: Unmanaged Form Data and Ambiguous Confirmation**\n   - **Evidence**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:34-35`, `FeedbackForm.jsx:61-63`, and `FeedbackForm.jsx:72-115`.\n   - **Element**: `<p className=\"status-message success\">Your submission was successful!</p>`\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Cognitive accessibility users, Screen reader users, Keyboard users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions (Level A), WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: After `status` transitions to `'success'`, the success message provides zero contextual details (no summary of what was sent, no recipient mention, no next step expectations). Worse, the `<form>` remains in the DOM directly below the message with all form fields (`formData.name`, `formData.email`, `formData.message`) completely populated and the submit button re-enabled as \"Send Feedback\". Screen reader and keyboard users who navigate forward encounter their existing inputs and an enabled submit button, inducing doubt as to whether the transaction completed or requires re-submission.\n   - **Fix**: On success, either:\n     - Clear the form fields and announce: *\"Thank you, your feedback has been sent. The form has been reset.\"*, OR\n     - Swap the form UI for a dedicated confirmation panel (`<div role=\"region\" aria-labelledby=\"success-heading\">`) containing an `<h2>` receiving programmatic focus (`tabIndex={-1}`) and a \"Send another message\" reset button.\n\n3. **Focus Outline Removal in High Contrast / Forced Colors Mode**\n   - **Evidence**: `FeedbackForm.css:78-80`.\n   - **Element**: `.form-group input:focus`, `.form-group textarea:focus`\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Low vision users, Windows High Contrast Mode users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.4.7 Focus Visible (Level A), WCAG 2.4.13 Focus Appearance (Level AA).\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: The CSS explicitly sets `outline: none` on input and textarea focus, replacing it with `border-color: #3b82f6` and `box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.15)`.\n     - In Windows High Contrast Mode / Forced Colors (`forced-colors: active`), `box-shadow` is completely discarded by the browser rendering engine.\n     - With `outline: none`, focused text inputs lose perimeter demarcation in forced colors mode.\n     - In standard mode, `rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.15)` has an opacity of 15%, which is too faint to guarantee perceptible contrast against adjacent surfaces (fails 3:1 contrast change per 2.4.13 against the `#cbd5e1` unfocused border).\n   - **Fix**: Use a dedicated outline with offset:\n     ```css\n     .form-group input:focus,\n     .form-group textarea:focus {\n       outline: 2px solid #2563eb;\n       outline-offset: 1px;\n     }\n     ```\n\n4. **Missing Programmatic Error Association and Input Validation State**\n   - **Evidence**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:38` (`setErrorMessage(...)`), `FeedbackForm.jsx:65-69`, and `FeedbackForm.jsx:73-106`.\n   - **Element**: `<p className=\"status-message error\">` and `<input>` / `<textarea>`\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen Reader users, Cognitive users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 3.3.1 Error Identification (Level A), WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A).\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: When `handleSubmit` catches an error, it renders a generic message at the top of the form. None of the form fields receive `aria-invalid=\"true\"`, and the error container has no `id` linked to the inputs or form via `aria-describedby`. If an AT user tabs into the inputs, they receive no indication that submission failed. Furthermore, \"Something went wrong. Please try again.\" provides no actionable recovery guidance (e.g., network timeout vs. invalid data).\n   - **Fix**: Add `id=\"form-status-message\"` to the message element, link inputs via `aria-describedby=\"form-status-message\"` when in an error state, and provide actionable recovery copy.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings\n\n1. **Unannounced Requirement Indicator (Missing Visual Indicator)**\n   - **Evidence**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:82, 95, 104` (inputs have `required`, but labels at lines `74, 87, 98` have no visual indicator).\n   - **Element**: `<label htmlFor=\"...\">Full Name</label>`, `<label htmlFor=\"...\">Email Address</label>`\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Cognitive accessibility users, Sighted keyboard users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions (Level A).\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: While the HTML5 `required` attribute communicates requirement programmatically to screen readers, there is no visual indicator (such as an asterisk `*` or text \"(required)\") in the `<label>`. Sighted users only discover fields are required when the browser blocks submission upon clicking the button.\n   - **Fix**: Add visual indicators to the labels, e.g., `<label ...>Full Name <span aria-hidden=\"true\">*</span><span className=\"sr-only\">(required)</span></label>`.\n\n2. **200ms State Blip Permits Form Double-Submission**\n   - **Evidence**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:32-35` (`setStatus('clearing')` followed by `setTimeout(..., 200)`).\n   - **Element**: `handleSubmit` async flow and `disabled={status === 'submitting'}`.\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Motor impaired and Cognitive users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 3.3.4 Error Prevention (Legal/Financial/Data).\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: The intermediate `'clearing'` status sets `status !== 'submitting'`, causing the submit button to re-enable for 200ms before `status` changes to `'success'`. A rapid double-click or fast keyboard activation during this window triggers a duplicate HTTP POST request.\n   - **Fix**: Ensure the button remains disabled or requests are blocked if `status === 'submitting' || status === 'clearing' || status === 'success'`.\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements\n\n1. **Missing Reduced-Motion Handling on Loading Spinner**\n   - **Evidence**: `FeedbackForm.css:55` (`animation: spin 0.8s linear infinite;`).\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Users with Vestibular and Motion sensitivities.\n   - **WCAG Citation**: WCAG 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide (Level A).\n   - **Fix**: Add `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .spinner { animation: none; border-top-color: currentColor; } }` or replace the rotating CSS spinner with a pulse or static loading indicator.\n\n2. **Suboptimal Submit Button Disabled Contrast**\n   - **Evidence**: `FeedbackForm.css:105-108` (`.submit-button:disabled { opacity: 0.6; }`).\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Low vision and Cognitive users.\n   - **Context**: White text on `#2563eb` with 60% opacity against a white card yields an effective contrast of ~2.5:1. Although WCAG 1.4.3 exempts disabled controls, maintaining legible text (e.g., `background: #94a3b8; color: #ffffff`) helps low vision users read \"Submitting...\" without strain.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing\n\n1. **Missing Programmatic Focus Movement**: `statusRef` is declared (`useRef(null)`) and assigned to the status container (`FeedbackForm.jsx:47`), but there is no `statusRef.current?.focus()` call in `handleSubmit` upon success or error.\n2. **Missing Form Reset / Dismiss Mechanism**: No option to reset the form or start a new submission once feedback has succeeded.\n3. **Missing Landmark / Form Identity**: The container lacks landmark scoping (e.g., `<section aria-labelledby=\"feedback-title\">` or `<form aria-labelledby=\"feedback-title\">`).\n4. **Missing Clear Contact / Alternative Path**: In the event of persistent server errors, there is no fallback contact email, phone number, or FAQ link.\n\n---\n\n## Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| **Screen reader** | **HIGH** | `aria-busy` silencing loading updates; conflicting live regions (`role=\"alert\"` inside `role=\"status\"`); unassociated form errors. |\n| **Keyboard-only** | **HIGH** | Focus lost to `document.body` when submit button disables; no focus management to status messages. |\n| **Low vision** | **MEDIUM** | `outline: none` coupled with weak 15% opacity `box-shadow`; complete outline loss in Windows High Contrast Mode. |\n| **Cognitive** | **HIGH** | Vague success copy; form fields remain populated after submission; no visible requirement indicators on mandatory fields. |\n| **Vestibular & motion** | **LOW** | Spinner is small (16px), but runs continuous 0.8s infinite rotation without `prefers-reduced-motion`. |\n| **Auditory access** | **LOW** | No media elements or audio notifications present. |\n| **Environmental contrast** | **LOW** | Standard text colors (`#1e293b`, `#15803d`, `#b91c1c`) meet 4.5:1 contrast against their respective backgrounds. |\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen Reader User**: Screen reader users will experience silent periods during submission because `aria-busy=\"true\"` suppresses the loading announcement. If submission succeeds, they hear the unspecific sentence \"Your submission was successful!\" but continuing in the virtual reading buffer presents the form still populated with their text, giving the impression that submission is incomplete.\n- **Keyboard-only User**: The moment the user hits Enter on the submit button, focus is ejected to the browser body. When the request resolves, the user must navigate through the entire page from the beginning. Tab will land in the first input instead of highlighting the status banner.\n- **Low Vision User**: Users who rely on high contrast themes will find that text inputs lose all focus demarcation due to `outline: none`. Users working in standard themes will find the 15% alpha glow insufficient to track which field has focus.\n- **Cognitive Accessibility**: Lack of clear field requirements upfront, generic error copy (\"Something went wrong\"), and a success state that fails to confirm recipient details or clear user data make the workflow disorienting.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n**Verdict: REVISE**\n\nThe component exhibits thoughtful intentions (using semantic tags, `autoComplete`, and `role=\"status\"`), but contains critical architectural gaps in focus management and state lifecycle. Automated linters and test suites will report passing scores, masking the fact that keyboard users lose focus to `<body>`, screen reader users have loading announcements suppressed by `aria-busy`, and cognitive users are presented with ambiguous post-submission state.\n\n**Prerequisites for an ACCEPT verdict:**\n1. Eliminate focus loss by either focusing the status container (`tabIndex={-1}`) or avoiding native `disabled` on the active trigger.\n2. Remove `aria-busy=\"true\"` from the live region.\n3. Decouple `role=\"alert\"` from `role=\"status\"` (avoid nesting live regions).\n4. Transition the UI on success to a distinct confirmation state, or clear the form and explicitly announce the reset.\n5. Restore a solid, visible focus outline that survives Windows High Contrast Mode (`forced-colors`).\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (unscored)\n1. *Post-submission behavior*: Is this form intended to allow immediate multiple submissions, or should it transition to a persistent \"Thank You\" screen with a \"Send another message\" action?\n2. *Server error granularity*: Can the API return field-specific validation errors (e.g., invalid email syntax, length violations) that should be mapped to specific inputs with `aria-invalid` and `aria-describedby`?\n",
